<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- The shared Avalonia toolkit: theme, controls, window scaffolding and a
       fluent layout surface, so EmuSen.Mistress, EmuSen.Hotaru and the future
       launcher stop rebuilding chrome from raw primitives. See
       EmuSen_LunaP.md for what exists and EmuSen_LunaP_Gameplan.md for what
       is planned; named for Luna-P, Chibiusa's gadget ball (naming scheme
       §11 - distinct from Luna, the reserved DS core codename).

       THE LAYERING RULE, and it is load-bearing: this project may reference
       Avalonia, EmuSen.Galaxia and EmuSen.Cauldron, and NOTHING ELSE. Not
       EmuSen (the core), not EmuSen.DianaOS, not EmuSen.Endymion. The
       launcher's whole value is browsing a library with no core loaded, and
       an upward reference to any of those hands it the entire emulator.
       EmuSen.Serenity already holds this line on the video side - it presents
       game frames while taking (byte[] rgba, int w, int h) rather than an
       ICore, and every CONTROL here takes plain data or a delegate for the
       same reason. See EmuSen_LunaP.md §1.

       Cauldron is on the list and the other three are not, because Cauldron
       is a dependency-free leaf: six files of read-only telemetry contracts,
       no PackageReference and no ProjectReference of its own. Referencing it
       costs the launcher one small interfaces assembly, not a core - which is
       the thing this rule exists to prevent. Dashboards/ is the only folder
       allowed to name it; a control that wants an ICoreTelemetry is a control
       that should have taken plain data. See EmuSen_LunaP.md §16. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>true</AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Versions pinned to match EmuSen.Mistress exactly, the convention
         EmuSen.Serenity's csproj already follows. Desktop/Fonts.Inter/X11 are
         here for LunaApp.Configure, which owns the UsePlatformDetect/
         WithInterFont/LogToTrace/UseX11 sequence both frontends' Program.cs
         used to spell out separately - see EmuSen_LunaP.md §3. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" Version="12.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Desktop" Version="12.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Markup.Xaml.Loader" Version="12.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" Version="12.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Fonts.Inter" Version="12.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.X11" Version="12.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- ConfigFile, which Windowing/WindowPlacementStore.cs persists remembered
         window geometry through. The only ProjectReference this project is
         allowed to have; see the layering rule above. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\EmuSen.Galaxia\EmuSen.Galaxia.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\EmuSen.Cauldron\EmuSen.Cauldron.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
